{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_index","statement":"Django provides django.contrib.staticfiles to help manage static files such as images, JavaScript, or CSS.","source_span":"Django provides :mod:`django.contrib.staticfiles` to help you manage them."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_index","statement":"django.contrib.staticfiles must be included in INSTALLED_APPS to configure static files.","source_span":"Make sure that ``django.contrib.staticfiles`` is included in your :setting:`INSTALLED_APPS`."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_index","statement":"An example STATIC_URL setting value is \"static/\".","source_span":"STATIC_URL = \"static/\""}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_index","statement":"Static files should be stored in a folder called \"static\" inside the app, e.g. my_app/static/my_app/example.jpg.","source_span":"Store your static files in a folder called ``static`` in your app. For example ``my_app/static/my_app/example.jpg``."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_index","statement":"During development, runserver automatically serves static files when DEBUG is set to True, if using django.contrib.staticfiles.","source_span":"this will be done automatically by :djadmin:`runserver` when :setting:`DEBUG` is set to ``True``"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_index","statement":"The automatic development static-file-serving method is grossly inefficient and probably insecure, so it is unsuitable for production.","source_span":"This method is **grossly inefficient** and probably **insecure**, so it is **unsuitable for production**."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_index","statement":"STATICFILES_DIRS can list extra static directories, e.g. BASE_DIR / \"static\" and \"/var/www/static/\".","source_span":"STATICFILES_DIRS = [\n        BASE_DIR / \"static\",\n        \"/var/www/static/\",\n    ]"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_index","statement":"Django uses the first static file it finds whose name matches, so files should be namespaced by app name to avoid name collisions between apps.","source_span":"Django will use the first static file it finds whose name matches, and if you had a static file with the same name in a *different* application, Django would be unable to distinguish between them."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_index","statement":"django.contrib.staticfiles.testing.StaticLiveServerTestCase is a subclass of LiveServerTestCase that can transparently serve all static assets during test execution without needing to run collectstatic first.","source_span":"staticfiles ships its own :class:`django.contrib.staticfiles.testing.StaticLiveServerTestCase`, a subclass of the built-in one that has the ability to transparently serve all the assets during execution of these tests ... without having to collect them using :djadmin:`collectstatic` first."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_index","statement":"An example STATIC_ROOT setting for deployment is \"/var/www/example.com/static/\".","source_span":"STATIC_ROOT = \"/var/www/example.com/static/\""}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_index","statement":"The static() helper function used in urls.py for serving files in development only works in debug mode and only if the prefix is local, not a full URL.","source_span":"This helper function works only in debug mode and only if the given prefix is local (e.g. ``static/``) and not a URL (e.g. ``http://static.example.com/``)."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_index","statement":"Static file requests served via the WSGI application wrapper do not pass through the normal middleware chain.","source_span":"static files are served via a wrapper at the WSGI application layer. As a consequence, static files requests do not pass through the normal :doc:`middleware chain"}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_index","fact_refs":[1,2],"question":"What two settings-file steps are required to configure static files in Django, and what's an example STATIC_URL value?","answer":"django.contrib.staticfiles must be included in INSTALLED_APPS, and STATIC_URL must be defined in the settings file, for example STATIC_URL = \"static/\"."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_index","fact_refs":[3],"question":"Where should static files be stored within a Django app, according to the docs?","answer":"In a folder called \"static\" inside the app, for example my_app/static/my_app/example.jpg."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_index","fact_refs":[4,5],"question":"Is Django's automatic static file serving during development suitable for production use?","answer":"No. During development, runserver automatically serves static files when DEBUG=True, but this method is grossly inefficient and probably insecure, making it unsuitable for production."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_index","fact_refs":[6],"question":"What are two example paths that can be added to the STATICFILES_DIRS setting?","answer":"BASE_DIR / \"static\" and \"/var/www/static/\"."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_index","fact_refs":[8],"question":"What is StaticLiveServerTestCase and what advantage does it offer over the standard LiveServerTestCase?","answer":"It's a subclass of LiveServerTestCase, provided by django.contrib.staticfiles.testing, that can transparently serve all static assets during test execution without needing to run collectstatic first."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_index","fact_refs":[10,11],"question":"What are two limitations of the static() helper function used in urls.py for serving files during development?","answer":"It only works in debug mode and only for a local URL prefix (not a full URL like http://static.example.com/); also, static file requests served this way bypass the normal middleware chain."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_upgrade-version","statement":"Older versions of Django eventually stop receiving security updates.","source_span":"Older versions of Django eventually stop receiving security updates"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_upgrade-version","statement":"When upgrading through more than one feature version (e.g. 2.0 to 2.2), it's usually easier to upgrade incrementally through each feature release (2.0 to 2.1 to 2.2) rather than all at once.","source_span":"If you're upgrading through more than one feature version (e.g. 2.0 to 2.2), it's usually easier to upgrade through each feature release incrementally (2.0 to 2.1 to 2.2)"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_upgrade-version","statement":"For each feature release upgraded through, use the latest patch release, e.g. for 2.1 use 2.1.15.","source_span":"For each feature release, use the latest patch release (e.g. for 2.1, use 2.1.15)."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_upgrade-version","statement":"In Python, deprecation warnings are silenced by default and must be enabled with the -Wa command line option or the PYTHONWARNINGS environment variable.","source_span":"In Python, deprecation warnings are silenced by default. You must turn them on using the ``-Wa`` Python command line option or the :envvar:`PYTHONWARNINGS` environment variable."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_upgrade-version","statement":"An example command to show deprecation warnings while running Django tests is: python -Wa manage.py test.","source_span":"$ python -Wa manage.py test"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_upgrade-version","statement":"An example pytest command to show warnings is: PYTHONWARNINGS=always pytest tests --capture=no.","source_span":"$ PYTHONWARNINGS=always pytest tests --capture=no"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_upgrade-version","statement":"To upgrade a pip-installed Django, use the --upgrade or -U flag, e.g. python -m pip install -U Django.","source_span":"If you installed Django with pip_, you can use the ``--upgrade`` or ``-U`` flag:\n\n.. console::\n\n   $ python -m pip install -U Django"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_upgrade-version","statement":"After upgrading, it's advisable to clear the cache because cached pickled objects are not guaranteed to be pickle-compatible across Django versions.","source_span":"you should consider clearing your cache after upgrading. Otherwise you may run into problems, for example, if you are caching pickled objects as these objects are not guaranteed to be pickle-compatible across Django versions."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_upgrade-version","statement":"A past instance of pickle incompatibility involved caching pickled HttpResponse objects, either directly or indirectly via the cache_page decorator.","source_span":"A past instance of incompatibility was caching pickled :class:`~django.http.HttpResponse` objects, either directly or indirectly via the :func:`~django.views.decorators.cache.cache_page` decorator."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_upgrade-version","fact_refs":[0],"question":"Why can't you keep using an old version of Django indefinitely, according to the upgrade guide?","answer":"Older versions of Django eventually stop receiving security updates."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_upgrade-version","fact_refs":[1,2],"question":"What's the recommended approach for upgrading Django across multiple feature versions, e.g. from 2.0 to 2.2?","answer":"Upgrade incrementally through each feature release (2.0 to 2.1 to 2.2) rather than all at once, using the latest patch release for each version, e.g. 2.1.15 for the 2.1 release."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_upgrade-version","fact_refs":[3,4],"question":"How do you enable deprecation warnings when running Django's test suite, since Python silences them by default?","answer":"Use the -Wa Python command line option or set the PYTHONWARNINGS environment variable, for example by running `python -Wa manage.py test`."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_upgrade-version","fact_refs":[5],"question":"How would you show deprecation warnings when running tests with pytest instead of Django's test runner?","answer":"Run `PYTHONWARNINGS=always pytest tests --capture=no`."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_upgrade-version","fact_refs":[6],"question":"What command upgrades an existing pip-installed Django to the latest version?","answer":"`python -m pip install -U Django`, using the --upgrade or -U flag."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_upgrade-version","fact_refs":[7,8],"question":"Why should you clear your cache after upgrading Django if you rely on Django's caching?","answer":"Because cached pickled objects aren't guaranteed to be pickle-compatible across Django versions — a past example was caching pickled HttpResponse objects, directly or via the cache_page decorator."}
