{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_windows","statement":"Django assumes UTF-8 encoding is used for I/O by default, and this can be forced on Windows via the PYTHONUTF8 environment variable.","source_span":"In general, Django assumes that ``UTF-8`` encoding is used for I/O... Recent versions of Python allow setting the :envvar:`PYTHONUTF8` environment variable in order to force a ``UTF-8`` encoding."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_windows","fact_refs":[0,1,2],"question":"Which Python and Windows versions does Django's Windows installation guide target?","answer":"The guide covers installing Python 3.14 (the latest version at time of writing) and Django on Windows, with steps tested on Windows 10."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_windows","fact_refs":[5,6],"question":"What commands create and activate a virtual environment named 'project-name' on Windows, per the Django install guide?","answer":"Create it with `py -m venv project-name`, then activate it with `project-name\\Scripts\\activate.bat`."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_windows","fact_refs":[7,8],"question":"How do you install Django on Windows and verify the installation succeeded?","answer":"Run `py -m pip install Django` inside the active virtual environment, then verify it with `django-admin --version`."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_windows","fact_refs":[9],"question":"How can you disable colored terminal output in Django on Windows?","answer":"Set the DJANGO_COLORS environment variable to \"nocolor\"."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_windows","fact_refs":[10],"question":"What's required to get colored syntax output on older Windows versions or legacy terminals?","answer":"colorama version 0.4.6 or newer must be installed."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_windows","fact_refs":[11],"question":"What encoding does Django assume for I/O, and how can you force Python to use it on Windows?","answer":"Django assumes UTF-8 encoding for I/O; this can be forced by setting the PYTHONUTF8 environment variable."}
